WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.718
One number, the rank

2
00:00:04.718 --> 00:00:08.181
sorts every system before you solve it.

3
00:00:08.181 --> 00:00:11.470
Compare r with m, the number of rows

4
00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:14.500
and with n, the number of columns.

5
00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:17.793
Draw each equation as a line in

6
00:00:17.888 --> 00:00:20.356
the plane of the unknowns.

7
00:00:20.356 --> 00:00:21.685
Square first

8
00:00:21.685 --> 00:00:24.817
x one plus two x two equals one

9
00:00:24.817 --> 00:00:26.906
and x two equals one.

10
00:00:27.001 --> 00:00:29.943
Two lines that are not parallel

11
00:00:30.038 --> 00:00:34.120
cross at exactly one point, minus one, one.

12
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.158
r equals m equals n equals two

13
00:00:37.158 --> 00:00:41.050
one solution, for every right hand side.

14
00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:45.469
Now the two to three layer: a third row

15
00:00:45.469 --> 00:00:47.772
x one plus three x two.

16
00:00:47.772 --> 00:00:50.747
With the target one, one, two

17
00:00:50.843 --> 00:00:53.530
the third line is x one plus

18
00:00:53.626 --> 00:00:55.833
three x two equals two.

19
00:00:55.833 --> 00:00:59.096
It passes through minus one, one

20
00:00:59.096 --> 00:01:01.879
minus one plus three is two.

21
00:01:01.879 --> 00:01:03.990
Exactly one solution.

22
00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:06.940
Change the last target to one.

23
00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:08.847
The third line moves

24
00:01:08.847 --> 00:01:12.487
and minus one plus three is two, not one

25
00:01:12.487 --> 00:01:15.087
it misses the crossing point.

26
00:01:15.087 --> 00:01:16.820
No solution at all.

27
00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:20.712
Tall, with r equal to n, two

28
00:01:20.712 --> 00:01:23.643
but less than m, three

29
00:01:23.643 --> 00:01:27.917
a zero row gives a condition on b

30
00:01:27.917 --> 00:01:30.970
so zero or one solution.

31
00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:34.348
Keep a faint copy of the layer.

32
00:01:34.348 --> 00:01:38.410
Now the matrix with rows one, three and two

33
00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:40.035
six, of rank one.

34
00:01:40.035 --> 00:01:42.291
With target four, eight

35
00:01:42.291 --> 00:01:45.902
the second equation is twice the first

36
00:01:45.902 --> 00:01:47.978
one line, drawn twice.

37
00:01:47.978 --> 00:01:50.776
Every point on it solves both

38
00:01:50.776 --> 00:01:52.310
infinitely many.

39
00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:54.565
With target four, nine

40
00:01:54.648 --> 00:01:56.884
the second line is parallel

41
00:01:56.967 --> 00:02:00.197
and never meets the first: no solution.

42
00:02:00.197 --> 00:02:03.013
When r is less than both m and n

43
00:02:03.013 --> 00:02:06.740
there are zero or infinitely many solutions.

44
00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.255
The last case needs three unknowns

45
00:02:10.255 --> 00:02:12.183
so we use the numbers.

46
00:02:12.267 --> 00:02:14.950
The data matrix has two examples

47
00:02:15.034 --> 00:02:16.543
and three weights.

48
00:02:16.543 --> 00:02:18.975
Its rank is two, equal to m

49
00:02:18.975 --> 00:02:20.400
and less than n.

50
00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:25.265
For the targets three, two, one fit is two

51
00:02:25.265 --> 00:02:26.945
one half, zero

52
00:02:26.945 --> 00:02:30.410
two plus one plus zero is three

53
00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:34.190
and two plus zero plus zero is two.

54
00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:36.705
The library finds another

55
00:02:36.705 --> 00:02:40.202
one point two two, zero point eight nine

56
00:02:40.202 --> 00:02:42.200
zero point seven eight.

57
00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:43.283
It fits too

58
00:02:43.366 --> 00:02:46.030
one point two two plus one point

59
00:02:46.114 --> 00:02:47.862
seven eight is three

60
00:02:47.945 --> 00:02:50.193
one point two two plus zero

61
00:02:50.277 --> 00:02:52.358
point seven eight is two.

62
00:02:52.358 --> 00:02:55.938
With r equal to m, every target is fitted

63
00:02:55.938 --> 00:02:58.020
in infinitely many ways.

64
00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:02.732
numpy's solve handles only the first case

65
00:03:02.732 --> 00:03:06.176
a square matrix with r equal to n.

66
00:03:06.176 --> 00:03:08.734
lstsq handles every case.

67
00:03:08.734 --> 00:03:12.867
When there are infinitely many solutions

68
00:03:12.867 --> 00:03:15.720
it returns the shortest one.

69
00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:18.480
Picture it on the solution line

70
00:03:18.558 --> 00:03:21.902
of the rank one example: x p is four, zero

71
00:03:21.979 --> 00:03:24.545
but the point of the line closest

72
00:03:24.623 --> 00:03:26.567
to the origin is shorter.

73
00:03:26.567 --> 00:03:29.988
For the drone, the least squares answer has

74
00:03:30.066 --> 00:03:32.165
length zero point six four

75
00:03:32.165 --> 00:03:35.353
while x p has length one point four one.

76
00:03:35.353 --> 00:03:36.753
Both are correct

77
00:03:36.753 --> 00:03:39.630
two points of the same solution set.

78
00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:43.759
A three by three matrix has rank two.

79
00:03:43.759 --> 00:03:47.884
Can A x equal b have exactly one solution?

80
00:03:47.884 --> 00:03:49.610
Pause and decide.

81
00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:50.436
No.

82
00:03:50.436 --> 00:03:52.093
r is less than n

83
00:03:52.093 --> 00:03:55.867
so there is a free variable: a solution

84
00:03:55.867 --> 00:03:59.089
when there is one, is never alone.

85
00:03:59.089 --> 00:04:01.115
And r is less than m

86
00:04:01.115 --> 00:04:03.600
so some targets have none.

87
00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:05.653
The opposite mistake

88
00:04:05.734 --> 00:04:08.086
exactly one solution does not

89
00:04:08.167 --> 00:04:09.870
need a square matrix.

90
00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:13.357
The tall example had exactly one solution

91
00:04:13.357 --> 00:04:15.790
for the target one, one, two.

92
00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:23.043
An assistant answers for the drone

93
00:04:23.151 --> 00:04:25.852
with x p and a nullspace.

94
00:04:25.852 --> 00:04:30.498
x p is one, one, zero, zero: A x p is two

95
00:04:30.498 --> 00:04:31.903
five, three.

96
00:04:31.903 --> 00:04:32.875
Correct.

97
00:04:32.875 --> 00:04:37.413
Its one nullspace vector, one, minus one

98
00:04:37.413 --> 00:04:39.898
zero, one, gives zero.

99
00:04:39.898 --> 00:04:41.410
Also correct.

100
00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.993
But count: n minus r is four minus two

101
00:04:44.993 --> 00:04:47.231
two directions are needed

102
00:04:47.231 --> 00:04:49.220
and only one was given.

103
00:04:49.303 --> 00:04:51.789
The answer misses every recipe

104
00:04:51.872 --> 00:04:53.447
that uses button w.

105
00:04:53.447 --> 00:04:56.514
Multiplying alone cannot catch that

106
00:04:56.514 --> 00:04:57.840
the count does.

107
00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:01.601
So: r equal to m equal to n

108
00:05:01.601 --> 00:05:05.463
two lines crossing: one solution.

109
00:05:05.463 --> 00:05:08.530
r equal to n, less than m

110
00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:12.620
a third line may miss, zero or one.

111
00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:17.819
r equal to m, less than n: infinitely many

112
00:05:17.819 --> 00:05:18.721
always.

113
00:05:18.721 --> 00:05:20.750
r less than both

114
00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:24.471
the same line or parallel lines

115
00:05:24.471 --> 00:05:27.290
zero or infinitely many.

116
00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:28.517
Your turn

117
00:05:28.517 --> 00:05:31.588
a two by five matrix has rank two.

118
00:05:31.675 --> 00:05:33.869
For how many targets b is

119
00:05:33.956 --> 00:05:36.062
A x equal to b solvable?

120
00:05:36.062 --> 00:05:38.518
Next lecture: independence

121
00:05:38.518 --> 00:05:40.360
basis and dimension.
